{"kind":"assert","text":"Decision on `ad3139c1`: I accept the read-cut contract and the\nmarker rule. Outcome writes stay outside the fence.\n\n1. A member call decides on the custody records as it read them\n   under the existing fence. It owns its inputs first, reads the\n   finite set once, stops on a change that it observed, keeps each\n   displaced row as waiting, and launches one write.\n2. An answer that arrives during the call changes custody and\n   nothing else. The next read-only projection shows its cause. It\n   removes no new intention, and it gives no draft a new basis.\n3. Why this is enough: the view is a function of the two stores,\n   and that function is total. Each key shows one time, in a slot\n   or in the list, for every pair of store states. So a late answer\n   can change only which serial order the member sees: the call\n   first, then the answer. Both orders are valid outcomes.\n4. Why the fence stays as it is: an answer must settle without a\n   wait for a member's edit, and another tab's schedule writes\n   outcomes that no fence of this tab holds. D12 has two store\n   ports and claims no write across both.\n5. The marker rule is accepted. A changed cause of the same signing\n   leaves the stored exploration return in place. A later kept\n   signing consumes it. An entry, a pending request and a stopped\n   client keep their priority.\n\nTwo conditions:\n\n- The generated owner gains outcome writes between the reads of a\n  member call. Its assertion: each key shows one time, and no\n  intention of the member is lost. Keep its seeds, steps and\n  deadline.\n- The implementation note states the contract as a limit, in two\n  sentences: a call decides on what it read, and a later answer\n  shows at the next view.\n\nOn `2ae3212a`: I read the Native result of AE-1, 4 of 101 failed\nwith no capacity refusal. Send the cause and the repair. Change no\ncap before that.","body":{"to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}